WEBVTT

s1.1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.227
A coding agent on a laptop works well for one person.

s1.2
00:00:04.227 --> 00:00:06.330
A team asks harder questions.

s1.3
00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:10.533
Each person runs agents in a different way, and nobody sees all of them.

s1.4
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:13.870
Who runs the agents, on which machine, and with which keys?

s1.5
00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.940
How do you follow them, steer them and review their work?

s1.6
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.952
How do you merge safely, and keep credentials away from agent code?

s2.1
00:00:21.452 --> 00:00:26.472
Symphony turns tickets into work orders, from Linear or a GitHub Projects board.

s2.2
00:00:26.472 --> 00:00:31.399
A webhook starts the agent. When the work is done, it opens a ready pull request.

s2.3
00:00:31.399 --> 00:00:36.284
Each attempt runs in its own pod. The tracker, App and chat keys stay outside.

s2.4
00:00:36.284 --> 00:00:41.359
You follow, steer or take over any agent from Tern, the CLI or MCP.

s2.5
00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:46.826
Two models review each pull request. A train merges only the head that CI tested.

s2.6
00:00:46.826 --> 00:00:51.601
Admins choose a model for each role, and store the API keys in one place.

s2.7
00:00:51.601 --> 00:00:57.151
After each ticket, a learnings pull request can teach the repository. A person merges it.

s2.8
00:00:57.151 --> 00:00:59.343
Notices go to Slack or Discord.

s2.9
00:00:59.343 --> 00:01:02.870
An assistant in chat answers questions and plans tickets.

s2.10
00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:07.729
A first-run setup checks each value against the real service before it saves it.

s3.1
00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:11.772
The vendor mocks follow each vendor's published specification.

s3.2
00:01:11.772 --> 00:01:17.397
Real agents passed all 8 scenarios with Linear and Slack, and 4 with GitHub and Discord.

s3.3
00:01:17.397 --> 00:01:22.079
The merger was certified on a real GitHub repository, with real CI.

s3.4
00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:25.745
Each defect that these runs found now has a regression test.

s3.5
00:01:25.745 --> 00:01:30.197
On kind, a full Kubernetes run took 275 seconds.

s3.6
00:01:30.197 --> 00:01:34.780
Symphony is open source under the MIT License. You host it yourself.

s3.7
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:40.710
Models use API keys or cloud credentials. There is no OAuth or subscription login.

s4.1
00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:45.679
It fits a team that plans work in tickets and reviews code in pull requests.

s4.2
00:01:45.679 --> 00:01:50.781
A repository can send disputed findings to a maintainer instead of the arbiter.

s4.3
00:01:50.781 --> 00:01:55.466
Your code must be on GitHub, and the tracker must be Linear or GitHub Projects.

s4.4
00:01:55.466 --> 00:01:59.321
You need Kubernetes. One VM with k3s is enough.

s4.5
00:01:59.321 --> 00:02:04.227
Local mode runs on one machine, with no container. Use it to try Symphony.

s4.6
00:02:04.227 --> 00:02:09.088
Linear, Slack and Discord were certified against mocks, not the real services.

s5.1
00:02:09.588 --> 00:02:13.337
Install the API and Redis with one kubectl command.

s5.2
00:02:13.337 --> 00:02:16.466
Then run symphony setup, or open Setup in Tern.

s5.3
00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:18.689
Each step is checked before it is saved.

s5.4
00:02:18.689 --> 00:02:21.879
The video list in the docs covers each part in depth.
